## Analysis

The current design implements a complex multiplier using the **naive 4-multiplier form**:
- `y_re = a*c - b*d`
- `y_im = a*d + b*c`

Synthesis on iCE40 (which has **no DSP blocks** — everything maps to LUT4s) means each 16×16 multiplier costs a large reduction tree. The `area_lut = 6836` is dominated by these four multipliers.

There is a classic algebraic identity (**Gauss / Karatsuba complex multiply**) that computes the *exact same* result using only **3 multiplications**:

```
k1 = c*(a+b)   = ac + bc
k2 = a*(d-c)   = ad - ac
k3 = b*(c+d)   = bc + bd

y_re = k1 - k3 = ac - bd     ✓
y_im = k1 + k2 = ad + bc     ✓
```

Since all arithmetic is exact integer (no rounding), **precision stays perfect (999)** while eliminating one full multiplier plus its reduction tree — roughly a 25% cut in the multiplier-dominated area. I keep the existing 1-stage handshake/pipeline structure unchanged so throughput is preserved, and use 17-bit pre-adders / 33-bit products so no intermediate ever overflows (all real results fit in <2³¹).

